**Title:** What is a Patent Bet: Complete UK Betting System Guide 2026 
**Description:** A patent bet turns three picks into seven UK bets: singles, doubles and a treble. Returns occur when one wins, unlike trixie or yankee bets. Popular for horse racing and football.
# Patent Bet Explained: How the UK Multiple Betting System Works
A patent bet combines three selections into seven individual bets, offering coverage across singles, doubles, and one treble. Popular in horse racing and football betting, this full-cover system provides returns even if only one selection wins, making it a flexible option for 2026.
## Patent Bet Definition: 7 Bets from 3 Selections
A Patent Bet is a full-cover betting system that transforms three individual selections into seven separate bets. The structure includes three singles, three doubles, and one treble, creating a safety net that delivers returns even if only one selection wins. This format differs from a standard accumulator, where all selections must succeed for any payout. The Patent covers every possible winning combination from the chosen trio.
The seven-bet breakdown works as follows: each of the three selections generates its own single bet, ensuring a return if that choice wins at odds above evens. The three doubles pair each selection with the other two, while the single treble combines all three. This construction means a bettor stakes seven units total—one unit per bet. A £1 Patent Bet costs £7, as each component receives an equal stake.
Horse racing traditionally hosts the majority of Patent Bets, though football markets now frequently feature this structure. The format serves bettors who back three selections but want downside protection. Unlike a Trixie Bet, which omits the singles and contains only four bets (three doubles and one treble), the Patent includes those crucial individual wagers. This distinction matters when confidence varies across selections.
The Patent also contrasts with a Yankee Bet, which expands the concept to four selections and eleven bets (six doubles, four trebles, one fourfold). The Yankee omits singles entirely, similar to the Trixie. The Patent's inclusion of singles makes it more forgiving for scenarios where only one or two selections win, while still offering accumulator-style returns when all three succeed. This balance between risk and reward defines the Patent's appeal in 2026.
## How a Patent Bet Works: Structure and Minimum Winners
Understanding the Patent Bet requires clarity on how three selections—labelled A, B, and C—produce seven bets. The singles component covers Selection A alone, Selection B alone, and Selection C alone. These three bets activate individually, meaning a winning selection at favourable odds generates a return regardless of the other two outcomes. If Horse A wins at 3/1 but Horses B and C lose, the bettor still collects on the single for A.
The doubles layer pairs the selections: one bet combines A and B, another combines A and C, and the third joins B and C. Each double requires both selections within it to win for a payout. If Horses A and B both win, the AB double pays out, alongside the singles for A and B. The doubles multiply the odds of their two components, creating higher returns than singles alone.
The treble forms the final bet, combining all three selections into one accumulator. All three must win for this bet to pay. When Horse A, Horse B, and Horse C all succeed, the treble multiplies the odds of all three, delivering the largest return within the Patent structure. Alongside this treble payout, the bettor also collects on all three singles and all three doubles, maximising total winnings.
Minimum winner scenarios clarify the Patent's value. With one winner, only that selection's single pays. If Horse A wins at 2/1 with a £1 stake, the return is £3 (£2 profit plus £1 stake), against a £7 total outlay. Two winners trigger two singles plus one double. If Horses A and B win, the bettor collects on singles A and B, plus the AB double. Three winners activate all seven bets, paying three singles, three doubles, and one treble. This structure mirrors patterns found in football betting, where Team A, Team B, and Team C can replace the horse racing selections, applying the same seven-bet logic.
## Calculating Patent Bet Returns: Worked Examples
Understanding potential returns helps bettors assess whether a Patent Bet offers value across different winning scenarios. The structure includes three Singles, three Doubles, and one Treble, creating seven separate bets that generate returns when one, two, or all three selections succeed. At £1 per bet, the total stake reaches £7, making it essential to calculate exact returns for each outcome.
### Returns When One Selection Wins
When only one selection wins, just a single bet pays out from the seven-bet structure. Using Horse A at 3/1 odds with a £1 stake, the return equals £4 (£3 profit plus £1 stake returned). With a £7 total stake, this scenario produces a £3 loss overall. For bettors to break even when only one selection wins, that horse needs odds above 6/1. Shorter odds require multiple winners to generate profit, which demonstrates why careful selection matters in Patent Bet construction across Horse Racing or Football markets.
### Returns When Two Selections Win
Two winning selections activate three bets: two Singles and one Double. If Horse A (3/1) and Horse B (5/2) both win, the Singles return £4 and £4.50 respectively. The Double combines both odds: (3/1 + 1) × (5/2 + 1) × £1 stake = 4 × 3.5 = £14. Total return reaches £22.50 from these three bets, delivering £15.50 profit against the £7 stake. This scenario shows how Doubles multiply odds together rather than simply adding them, creating significantly better returns than Singles alone.
### Maximum Returns: All Three Selections Win
When all three selections succeed, every bet component pays out. Adding Horse C at 2/1 to the previous winners generates returns from three Singles (£4 + £4.50 + £3), three Doubles (A+B £14, A+C £12, B+C £10.50), and one Treble. The Treble multiplies all three odds: 4 × 3.5 × 3 × £1 = £42. Total return: £90 from the £7 stake, producing £83 profit. Compared to a standard Treble accumulator, a Patent Bet provides identical Treble returns but adds safety through Singles and Doubles, answering whether **is patent bet worth it** for those seeking balanced risk across Yankee Bet or Trixie Bet alternatives.
| Scenario | Winning Bets | Calculation | Total Return | Profit |
|----------|--------------|-------------|--------------|--------|
| One winner (Horse A 3/1) | 1 Single | £1 × 4 | £4.00 | -£3.00 |
| Two winners (A+B) | 2 Singles + 1 Double | £4 + £4.50 + £14 | £22.50 | £15.50 |
| Three winners (A+B+C) | 3 Singles + 3 Doubles + 1 Treble | £11.50 + £36.50 + £42 | £90.00 | £83.00 |
## Each Way Patent Bets: Doubling Your Stake for Place Coverage
An each way Patent Bet doubles the bet count to 14, covering both win and place outcomes for all seven bet components. This structure costs £14 at £1 per bet (£7 win, £7 place), providing returns even when selections finish in placed positions rather than winning outright. Each way terms in Horse Racing typically pay 1/4 or 1/5 of the win odds for the top two, three, or four finishers depending on field size.
The 14-bet structure breaks down as follows:
1. Three win Singles plus three place Singles
2. Three win Doubles plus three place Doubles 
3. One win Treble plus one place Treble
When a horse finishes second at 4/1 odds with 1/4 place terms, the place portion returns £2 (4/1 ÷ 4 = 1/1, paying £2 including stake). This place coverage proves valuable when one or two selections narrowly miss winning but still finish in paying positions. Combined with **Best Odds Guaranteed** at UK bookmakers, each way patents on Horse Racing provide protection against late odds movements while maintaining place insurance.
The doubled stake requires careful consideration in Football markets where each way betting typically applies only to specific tournaments with multiple place-paying positions. Compared to standard Trixie Bet or Yankee Bet structures, the Patent Bet offers Singles protection, making the each way option particularly suitable when confidence varies across the three selections rather than backing all equally for outright victories.
## Patent vs Trixie vs Yankee: Choosing the Right Multiple Bet
When selecting a multiple bet structure, understanding the differences between a Patent Bet, Trixie Bet, and Yankee Bet helps bettors match their strategy to their confidence level and budget. Each bet type offers distinct advantages depending on risk tolerance and the number of selections a bettor wants to combine.
| Bet Type | Selections | Total Bets | Minimum Winners | Stake (£1 unit) |
|----------|-----------|------------|-----------------|-----------------|
| Patent | 3 | 7 | 1 | £7 |
| Trixie | 3 | 4 | 2 | £4 |
| Yankee | 4 | 11 | 2 | £11 |
The Patent Bet provides the greatest safety net among these options. With three Singles included alongside three Doubles and one Treble, a Patent Bet returns a profit if just one selection wins at decent odds. This structure suits bettors with moderate confidence who want protection against multiple losses. Horse Racing punters frequently choose patents when backing three fancied horses across different races.
The Trixie Bet eliminates Singles to reduce the stake from £7 to £4, but requires at least two winners to generate returns. This bet appeals to bettors confident that multiple selections will win but seeking lower upfront costs. Football accumulators often use the Trixie structure when combining three match results.
The Yankee Bet expands to four selections with 11 total bets (six Doubles, four Trebles, one four-fold accumulator). While offering greater potential returns through more combinations, the £11 stake and two-winner minimum make Yankees suitable for bettors spreading risk across more events or seeking substantial profits from moderately confident selections.
Bettors with high confidence in all selections may prefer straight accumulators, while those seeking diversification with moderate confidence typically choose patents.
## Sports and Markets Where Patent Bets Excel
Horse Racing remains the traditional home of the Patent Bet, where the structure originated and continues to thrive. Punters frequently combine three horses running in separate races at the same meeting or across different courses, taking advantage of the safety net that Singles provide. The each-way patent option particularly suits horse racing, where place returns cushion narrow misses.
Football offers diverse applications for patent betting. Match result markets allow bettors to combine three home wins, away wins, or mixed outcomes across different fixtures. Both teams to score markets and player goal scorer selections work effectively within the patent structure, particularly when events occur independently across separate matches. The Singles component ensures returns even if only one match unfolds as predicted.
Tennis and golf outright markets provide suitable patent betting opportunities when backing three players in separate tournaments or three competitors within the same field. The independent nature of individual performances aligns with the patent structure's strengths, as each selection succeeds or fails based on distinct factors rather than correlated outcomes.
Patents work best when selections represent independent events. Combining three outcomes from the same match (for example, correct score, first goal scorer, and total goals) introduces correlation that undermines the diversification benefit. Similarly, backing three horses in the same race creates mutual exclusivity that defeats the patent's protective design. The Doubles and Treble components generate value when multiple independent selections succeed simultaneously.
## When to Use a Patent Bet: Strategic Scenarios
A patent bet works best in specific betting contexts where bettors seek a balance between potential returns and downside protection. Understanding when this bet type offers genuine value helps punters make informed wagering decisions.
The ideal scenario for what is a patent bet involves selecting three picks with moderate confidence levels, typically at odds ranging from 2/1 to 5/1. Horse Racing enthusiasts often deploy this structure when backing three runners across different meetings, whilst Football bettors may combine three weekend matches where outcomes seem likely but not certain. The patent bet structure provides insurance against a single failed selection destroying the entire stake.
This bet type suits those managing their betting bank conservatively. The singles component ensures some return even if only one selection wins, making it appropriate for testing value picks without the full risk exposure of a Trixie Bet or Yankee Bet, which require multiple winners to generate returns.
However, patent bets prove less suitable in certain situations. When dealing with very short odds favourites, the singles barely cover the stake, making the additional doubles and treble components ineffective. Bettors with high confidence in all three selections may find a straight treble accumulator delivers better value. For those with limited bankroll, simpler bet types like singles or doubles offer more controlled risk exposure.
The question "is patent bet worth it" depends entirely on individual circumstances: confidence levels, odds available, and risk tolerance all influence whether this seven-bet combination serves a bettor's strategic objectives effectively.
## Advantages and Disadvantages of Patent Bets
What is a patent bet offers distinct benefits and drawbacks that bettors must weigh before committing their stake. Understanding both sides helps punters determine whether this bet structure aligns with their wagering strategy and risk appetite.
### Key Advantages of Patent Betting
The primary benefit lies in downside protection through the singles component. Even if only one selection wins, the bettor receives a return, unlike accumulator-only structures that require multiple winners. This insurance value makes patent bets attractive for testing value picks where confidence levels vary across selections.
Multiple return paths exist within the seven-bet structure. Two winners trigger returns from both singles plus one double, whilst three winners activate all seven components. This flexibility suits moderate confidence scenarios where outcomes appear probable but not certain, providing partial returns across different result combinations.
### Limitations and Drawbacks
The seven-unit stake requirement represents a significant cost consideration. A £1 unit patent bet costs £7 total, which may strain smaller betting banks. This higher outlay must be justified by the added insurance value.
Maximum potential returns fall below equivalent treble accumulators. The singles and doubles components dilute the overall profit when all three selections win, as stake gets distributed across lower-odds combinations. For bettors confident in all picks, this trade-off may not deliver optimal value.
Calculation complexity can confuse newcomers unfamiliar with combination betting structures. Understanding how returns generate across different winning scenarios requires more knowledge than straightforward single bets. Whether is patent bet worth it depends on whether the insurance benefits outweigh these structural costs for individual betting situations.
## Common Patent Betting Mistakes to Avoid
Understanding what is a patent bet requires recognising where punters commonly go wrong. One frequent error involves selecting extremely short odds across all three selections. Unless each selection exceeds evens (2.00), single-win returns rarely cover the £7 stake, making the bet unprofitable even with one winner.
Many bettors also ignore correlation between selections. Choosing three favourites from the same race creates a scenario where if one loses, the others likely fail too—linked outcomes reduce patent bets' protective value.
Misunderstanding each way implications causes costly mistakes. Players forget that each way patents require 14 bets (£14 minimum stake for £1 units), then discover place terms don't justify the doubled outlay. Checking place odds and number of places paid before committing prevents disappointment.
Another common pitfall involves using patents when confidence levels suggest simpler bet types. If genuinely certain about all three selections winning, a straightforward treble offers better returns without covering partial outcomes that may never materialise.
When asking **is patent bet worth it**, consider these mistakes. Patent bets suit scenarios with genuine uncertainty across multiple events, not as blanket solutions for every accumulator situation. Responsible gambling means matching bet type to actual confidence levels and understanding exact costs involved.
## Frequently Asked Questions
### Can I place a patent bet on three horses in the same race?
Placing a patent bet on three horses in a single race defeats the bet structure's protective design. Since only one horse can win the race, the doubles and treble components become impossible to win, leaving only the singles active. This creates mutual exclusivity that eliminates the diversification benefit patents provide. The structure works best when selections come from different races or independent events where multiple outcomes can succeed simultaneously. For betting multiple horses in one race, standard win or each way singles deliver better value than forcing them into a patent format.
### What happens if all three selections in my patent bet lose?
When all three selections lose, every component of the patent bet fails and the entire £7 stake is lost. No returns occur from singles, doubles, or the treble since none of the bets contain a winning selection. This total loss scenario represents the maximum downside risk and highlights why selecting reasonable odds matters. The protection patents offer only activates when at least one selection wins, making careful pick evaluation essential before committing the seven-unit stake across all bet components.
### What minimum odds do I need on one selection to break even in a patent bet?
To break even when only one selection wins in a patent bet, that pick requires odds above 6/1. At £1 per bet with a £7 total stake, a single winner at 6/1 returns £7, matching the outlay exactly. Lower odds like 3/1 or 5/2 produce losses when just one selection succeeds, as shown in the worked examples where a 3/1 winner returns only £4 against the £7 cost. This mathematical threshold explains why patent bets suit moderate-to-longer odds selections rather than short-priced favourites, where multiple winners become necessary for profitability.
### Why is it called a patent bet and where did the name originate?
The patent bet name derives from traditional UK horse racing betting terminology, though the exact historical origin remains unclear in modern usage. The term likely references the 'patented' or standard nature of this seven-bet combination structure, distinguishing it from custom accumulator builds. Unlike Trixie or Yankee bets which have colourful naming origins, the patent label emphasises the systematic, formulaic approach of converting three selections into a fixed pattern of singles, doubles, and one treble. The terminology became established through widespread adoption in UK betting shops during the late 20th century.
### Do all UK bookmakers accept patent bets?
All major UK bookmakers accept patent bets as a standard multiple betting option, both online and in betting shops. Operators like Bet365, William Hill, Ladbrokes, and Paddy Power include patents in their bet slip interfaces alongside other combination bets. The structure appears automatically when adding three selections and selecting the patent option from the multiples menu. Some bookmakers apply maximum stake limits to multiples, but these typically affect high-stakes bettors rather than recreational punters using standard unit sizes. The widespread availability reflects the patent's established status in UK betting culture, particularly for horse racing and football markets.
### Can I use free bets or bonuses on patent bets?
Free bet eligibility for patent bets varies by bookmaker and bonus terms. Many UK operators restrict free bets to single wagers or specific accumulator types, excluding combination structures like patents. When free bets are permitted on multiples, they typically apply to the total stake rather than individual bet components, meaning a £7 free bet covers the entire patent rather than £1 per unit. Bonus funds often carry similar restrictions, with wagering requirements calculated on the total stake placed. Check specific terms before attempting to use promotional credits on patent bets, as violations can void winnings or forfeit bonus balances entirely.
### Is a patent bet better than placing three separate singles?
Patent bets and three separate singles serve different strategic purposes with distinct risk-reward profiles. Three singles cost £3 total and guarantee returns from each winner, providing simplicity and full control over individual stakes. A patent costs £7 but adds three doubles and one treble, creating accumulator-style returns when multiple selections win. The trade-off involves higher upfront cost against potential for multiplied odds through combinations. Bettors confident in multiple winners but seeking insurance against one failure find patents worthwhile, whilst those preferring lower stakes or treating each pick independently should stick with singles. Neither approach is universally superior — the choice depends on confidence levels and bankroll management preferences.
### What stake should I use per unit in a patent bet?
Unit stake selection depends on bankroll size and risk tolerance, with £1 per unit representing a common starting point for the £7 total cost. Conservative bettors often use 1-2% of their betting bank as the total patent stake, meaning a £350 bankroll suggests £3.50 to £7 total or 50p to £1 per unit. Higher stakes suit larger bankrolls or strong confidence in multiple winners, whilst smaller units protect against the total loss scenario when all selections fail. Many UK bookmakers accept stakes from 10p per unit upward, allowing flexible adjustment based on individual circumstances. The seven-unit structure means stake decisions carry more weight than with single bets, requiring careful consideration of potential loss versus return expectations.